go with health and  here's where the end of one approach  makes a difference because he would have  qualified as somebody that you might not  want to spend too much time and you  might think it's a waste of time and  money to do anything more than just a  superficial annual exam but this  gentleman was headed towards poor health  we also did a vo2 max test which would  give us insight into something called  metabolic equivalence which is a very  strong predictor of morbidity and  mortality and he looked very good that  way and with some intervention even  early on we actually improved his  metabolic equivalents significantly  within a few months but here's his CT  angiogram and a CT calcium score and he  was already in the seventy-fifth to 90th  percentile due to the amount of calcium  in his coronary arteries and he had some  significant stenosis meaning there was a  readi plaque buildup this was not good  news for a 47 year old and this is the  not in this particular gentleman because  he already wanted to intervene but this  is the reason you might see a marathoner  who thinks he's doing everything right  died of a heart attack because there is  changes there are changes that go on  beneath the surface that lead to disease  and when you don't collect data that's  precise and marry it to the story the  way we look at end of one data in each  individual person and when you look to  just averaging the mean and being  somebody who looks this good must be  healthy you are going to miss disease  and as we age all of us are going to  have disorders of Aging  so the end of one programming for those  of us who are familiar and many of you  will be familiar with an end of one it's  developing a data informed intervention  whether it's using sleep or modalities  that are quite conservative but can  change radically the the course of your  health trajectories for those of our  patients or as we like to think of them  clients because we don't want them ill  nutrition exercise or doing more  advanced testing to get holistic deeper  or wearables which is something that we  also spoke to with this gentleman  supplements or medications we want to  intervene to make sure that we have  progress and in this particular  gentleman we are interested in doing  some intervention and he was able to  choose a few things that made a  difference for example we suggested that  we discontinue intermittent vesting and  we try to ketogenic diet we reduced to  strength training and we increased more  intermittent aerobic and steady cardio  to minimize further increases in muscle  we also suggested that he start a  hormonal intervention to utilize his  natural physiology something called  human chorionic and in tropen we've  started him on D vitamin we also started  on some omegas and cocoa flavanols to  increase memory which was also an issue  we gave him root of astragalus because  that would help lengthen telomeres his  excellent data and we gave him a  prescription medication to help abate  his desire to smoke so there were a  number of interventions that helped move  him in the right direction fairly  rapidly and he was on board with almost  all of it we also because he was  interested in owning some of his own  information he was fascinated by the  notion of a wearable so we placed a CGM  on him which was able to look at the  sugar content in his body of foods that  he ate and we could assess this using  different devices the particular device  we used is placed on the tricep and it  looks at patterns and when you eat and  each individual has quite a different  pattern and it's fascinating to people  to get this information at the point of  eating instead of waiting for data to  come back and just getting one moment in  time through a fast and so he was able  to do just this and change the patterns  for himself I want to share another  study with you that actually looked at  metabolic typing this was reported in  cell and showed that you can take many  individuals I think this study was done  in about a thousand individuals and if I  point to the upper right hand side as  you're facing the slide you'll see that  this particular participant number four  forty five when eating a banana there  was a rise in sugar but when eating up  he is shown by the blue line the banana  is in the yellow line quite  appropriately the banana cause the sugar  to go up higher than the cookie but if  you compare that individual to the other  participant number six for for the  reverse happened the cookie cause the  sugar to go up whereas the banana did  not so you can see the when you look at  averages of people you squash  differences you can't really discern  what a particular individual  is going to do I've been wearing one of  these monitors despite not being a  diabetic for about three years now and  it's completely changed as an  endocrinologist that I am my  understanding of my own function and I  can't urge all of us enough to  understand that end of one approach to  figuring out how our bodies function is  going to be the medicine of the future  here you could see that leveraging and  of one data and patterns over time is  vital and there's a number of ways we  look at it and we do a deep dive into  each of the individuals that come to the  center ultimately although much of what  we do is telephonic and at a distance  and the changes that happen over time  can really make a difference his PSA  here just to pick out a couple of  numbers stayed steady despite what we  were doing which is excellent we want  that to change number of his variables  did change over time and we adjust  accordingly I would like to talk for a  moment about how we think of the nf1  approach and this to me goes way back a  couple of decades and it rests in the  fact that anyone essentially starts with  observing as we have thousands of  individuals and collecting a data  designed as a clinical investigative  trial and as you do that you're looking  at data that forms patterns enough folks  fall into certain patterned responses  and as you collect that data you're able  to observe it look at it and over time  formulate the population theory in  contrast to collecting lots and lots of  data the way the NIH is approaching it  looking at a million lives and then  theorizing how to bring that down to the  individual in fact in that paper that I  started out with in nature by Nicolas  store in April of 2015 he describes what  I've been doing here at the Center for  two decades in New York for a slightly  over a decade about 13 years now and  what Nicholas stork actually wrote out  is that this n of one approach  essentially starts out small and focused  and then works its way towards insights  that would immediately benefit a much  larger group and that's what we have  found because I want to stress it of  course you can't study globally what we  need to determine to help everybody in  the way we're doing it the center one at  a time  but we have plans to scale this kind of  approach into an app that will allow us  to offer this kind of approach broadly  and here you can see what we intend to  do is take the patterns that we have now  been studying and look at the  individuals where the green or pink or  blue collect all the essential data that  we want to collect offer it up through  the app and offer it globally so that by  seeing and quantifying this data and  marrying it to the particular story of  an individual we'd be able to evoke  change that would have an individual  change their path so that they can own  their own health destiny I hope this  makes sense it's been fun to talk about  what we've been doing here in New York  thank you if you feel interested in  contacting us please feel free both to  send an email here and I'm happy to  answer through this presentation if  you'd like to reach us beyond this you  can feel free to write to us through the  website at info at comte mtv.com thank  you very much
 

